---
/**
 * website/src/components/CampaignHeroArt.astro — the campaign hero's picture (the owner's
 * feedback of 2026-10-30; reconciliation F14).
 *
 * **What it draws.** One picture, read left to right: a task, the short-lived cloud job it
 * starts, and the pull request the job ends in — the product's own promise, drawn rather than
 * written. The job is drawn as a cloud with a live spinner inside it and a dashed lifetime
 * ring around it, so the middle of the picture is a thing that is starting up and will not be
 * there afterwards; the dashed route either side of it is the work going in and the pull
 * request coming out, and the third card on the right is that pull request, drawn as a merge
 * into a base branch and marked with the palette's one "this finished" colour.
 *
 * **It is drawn, not fetched.** Everything is inline SVG in this file's own markup: no raster
 * file, no icon set, no font, no filter and no `<use>` pointing anywhere else, so the picture
 * costs the page one block of markup and no request at all (F14, Global Constraint 8). The
 * colours come from the palette through the classes in the stylesheet below rather than from
 * values in the markup, exactly as the run diagram does it, so no colour is written twice and
 * nothing here is a literal the palette does not carry (W1.2).
 *
 * **The tone is the variant's, twice over: in the markup and in the paint.** `data-tone` on the
 * `<svg>` is the variant's own word from `website/data/landing.yaml` (spec §6), and the four
 * rules below paint the drawing's accent from it — green for `/save`, whose subject is what a
 * run does not cost; the site's structural blue for `/control`, whose subject is the policy; the
 * brightest cyan for `/scale`, where the same job is asked for many times at once; and green
 * again for `/crew`, whose register the spec asks to be lighter. `playful` is lighter in the
 * drawing rather than in a fourth colour: `--warn` is the palette's one "pay attention" tone and
 * belongs to the caps page, so `/crew`'s lightness is spent on the motif — the two sleeping
 * marks and the moon over the cloud — and on rounder, thicker strokes. A tone whose word the
 * data file changes paints the drawing the same way with another accent and another motif, and
 * no page types its own colour.
 *
 * **The four motifs are the four campaign's ideas, and each is one small group.** `/save` bears
 * a percent mark, `/control` a shield with a check inside it, `/scale` three lanes of the same
 * line, and `/crew` the sleeping marks and the moon. They sit over the cloud, in the one place
 * every variant leaves free, so the picture is one drawing with four registers rather than four
 * drawings.
 *
 * **Nothing is typed inside it, and it is named for the reader who is not shown it.** There is
 * no `<text>` anywhere in the drawing: every word on a campaign page comes from a data file
 * (G2.1), and a label set inside the picture would be a second copy of one of them that no page
 * and no data file holds — `website/test/landing-pages.mjs` reads the drawing and refuses one.
 * The `<svg>` is `role="img"` with an `aria-label` beside it, because the picture is the page's
 * own argument rather than decoration: a reader who is not shown it is told what it says, once.
 *
 * **The motion is three quiet loops, and CSS only.** The spinner turns, the cloud floats, the
 * route's dashes drift and the trail dies away — no script, no scroll tie, nothing that moves a
 * reader's eye off the headline. `Base.astro`'s one reduced-motion rule neutralises all four for
 * a visitor who asked their system for less motion (D105), which is why they need no
 * `@media (prefers-reduced-motion)` block of their own; `website/test/a11y.mjs` is the gate that
 * keeps that true.
 *
 * **Where it lands, and what takes its place.** The picture is the hero's own and sits inside
 * `CampaignHero.astro`, under the headline and the sentence on a phone and beside them from the
 * wide breakpoint. It is not a variant's media: `[variant].astro` renders the file a variant
 * names — its video or its image — in the layout's `media` slot at the top of the page, and a
 * variant that has one does not render this drawing at all (F14, G2.5).
 */
interface Props {
  /** The variant's tone (`savings`, `control`, `scale`, `playful`), from `landing.yaml`. */
  tone: string
}

const { tone } = Astro.props

// What the picture is, for a reader who is not shown it. It says what the drawing says and
// nothing the page does not promise: one task, one short-lived job, one pull request. "Job" and
// not "worker": a run's unit of work is a job, and the other word is retired from the site's
// copy (`website/test/wording.mjs`).
const ALT = 'An illustration: a task starts a short-lived cloud job, and the job ends in a pull request.'
---

<svg
  class="campaign-hero-art"
  data-tone={tone}
  viewBox="0 0 480 320"
  role="img"
  aria-label={ALT}
  preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
>
  {/* The panel the drawing sits on: one step above the ground and below the cards, so the
      picture reads as a thing laid on the page rather than as the page itself. */}
  <rect class="art-panel" x="1" y="1" width="478" height="318" rx="18" />

  {/* The task, on the left: a prompt mark and three lines of a request nobody has run yet. */}
  <rect class="art-card" x="26" y="84" width="122" height="104" rx="14" />
  <path class="art-mark" d="M44 104 l12 12 -12 12" />
  <path class="art-line" d="M68 116 h50" />
  <path class="art-line" d="M44 146 h86" />
  <path class="art-line" d="M44 164 h60" />

  {/* The route in: the task is handed to the job, which is the dashed line arriving at it. */}
  <path class="art-route" d="M152 136 h34" />
  <path class="art-arrow" d="M180 130 l7 6 -7 6" />

  {/* The job itself: a cloud that is starting up — a spinner inside it, a dashed lifetime ring
      around it, a trail of dots behind it — and the particles it throws off as it comes up. */}
  <circle class="art-ring" cx="242" cy="136" r="44" />
  <g class="art-cloud">
    <path class="art-cloud-shape" d="M208 150 A15 15 0 0 1 208 120 A19 19 0 0 1 242 112 A17 17 0 0 1 274 126 A13 13 0 0 1 274 150 Z" />
    <circle class="art-spin" cx="242" cy="134" r="9" />
  </g>
  <g class="art-trail">
    <circle class="art-dot" cx="196" cy="106" r="3" />
    <circle class="art-dot" cx="185" cy="124" r="2.2" />
    <circle class="art-dot" cx="194" cy="152" r="2.6" />
  </g>
  <path class="art-spark" d="M206 100 v11 M200.5 105.5 h11" />
  <path class="art-spark" d="M285 158 v9 M280.5 162.5 h9" />

  {/* The tone's own motif, in the one place every variant leaves free. */}
  <g class="art-motif" data-motif={tone}>
    {tone === 'savings' && (
      <>
        {/* A tenth of the price: the percent mark, drawn as its two dots and its slash. */}
        <circle class="art-motif-line" cx="230" cy="68" r="5.5" />
        <circle class="art-motif-line" cx="256" cy="92" r="5.5" />
        <path class="art-motif-line" d="M262 58 l-38 42" />
      </>
    )}
    {tone === 'control' && (
      <>
        {/* One policy over every run: a shield, and the check inside it. */}
        <path class="art-motif-line" d="M242 50 l18 8 v15 c0 11 -8 19 -18 24 -10 -5 -18 -13 -18 -24 V58 z" />
        <path class="art-motif-line" d="M234 77 l6 7 12 -15" />
      </>
    )}
    {tone === 'scale' && (
      <>
        {/* Many runs at once: the same line, three lanes deep. */}
        <path class="art-lane art-lane-1" d="M212 56 h60" />
        <path class="art-lane art-lane-2" d="M204 70 h76" />
        <path class="art-lane art-lane-3" d="M212 84 h60" />
      </>
    )}
    {tone === 'playful' && (
      <>
        {/* Somewhere else while it works: two sleeping marks over the cloud, and the moon. */}
        <path class="art-motif-line art-nap" d="M222 56 h20 l-20 16 h20" />
        <path class="art-motif-thin art-nap" d="M250 80 h13 l-13 11 h13" />
        <path class="art-motif-line art-moon" d="M284 50 a13 13 0 1 0 11 21 a10 10 0 1 1 -11 -21 z" />
      </>
    )}
  </g>

  {/* The route out, and the pull request it arrives at: merged into the base branch, marked
      with the palette's "this finished" colour. */}
  <path class="art-route" d="M290 136 h36" />
  <path class="art-arrow" d="M320 130 l7 6 -7 6" />
  <rect class="art-pr" x="334" y="84" width="120" height="104" rx="14" />
  <circle class="art-pr-node" cx="358" cy="136" r="8" />
  <path class="art-pr-merge" d="M370 136 h26" />
  <path class="art-arrow" d="M390 130 l7 6 -7 6" />
  <path class="art-pr-base" d="M418 118 v36" />
  <path class="art-check" d="M422 100 l4 5 9 -11" />
</svg>

<style>
  /* The drawing takes the width it is given and keeps its own proportions: it scales to the
     hero's column on every viewport rather than being sized in pixels here. */
  .campaign-hero-art {
    /* The tone's accent, until one of the rules below names the variant's own. It is a token
       and not a colour: every value in this file is one of the palette's. */
    --art-accent: var(--accent-on-dark);
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .art-panel {
    fill: var(--panel);
    stroke: var(--border);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .art-card,
  .art-pr {
    fill: var(--surface);
    stroke: var(--border);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  /* The pull request's card is the one thing on the page drawn in the "this finished" colour:
     the run ends there, and the palette has one colour that means it. */
  .art-pr {
    stroke: var(--ok);
  }

  .art-mark,
  .art-route,
  .art-arrow,
  .art-cloud-shape,
  .art-ring,
  .art-spin,
  .art-motif-line,
  .art-spark {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--art-accent);
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  .art-mark {
    stroke-width: 6;
  }

  .art-line {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--text-muted);
    stroke-width: 6;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    opacity: 0.45;
  }

  /* The route, in and out: a dashed line a reader reads as work travelling rather than as a
     border, and the arrowhead at the end of each one. */
  .art-route {
    stroke-width: 3;
    stroke-dasharray: 8 8;
    animation: art-flow 6s linear infinite;
  }

  .art-arrow {
    stroke-width: 3;
  }

  .art-ring {
    stroke-width: 2;
    stroke-dasharray: 6 12;
    opacity: 0.4;
  }

  .art-cloud-shape {
    /* The cloud is filled with its own accent at a low opacity, which is what makes it a body
       rather than an outline without adding a colour the palette does not carry. */
    fill: var(--art-accent);
    fill-opacity: 0.16;
    stroke-width: 3;
  }

  .art-cloud {
    animation: art-float 7s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  /* The spinner: a ring drawn three quarters empty, turning. Nothing about it is a picture of
     progress — the job is starting up — so it is deliberately not a bar a reader could read a
     percentage off. */
  .art-spin {
    stroke-width: 3;
    stroke-dasharray: 15 42;
    animation: art-spin 2.4s linear infinite;
  }

  .art-dot {
    fill: var(--art-accent);
    opacity: 0.45;
    animation: art-fade 5s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  .art-dot:nth-child(2) {
    animation-delay: 1.1s;
  }

  .art-dot:nth-child(3) {
    animation-delay: 2.2s;
  }

  /* The particles the job throws off as it comes up: two small crosses, faint enough to be
     texture rather than a second subject. */
  .art-spark {
    stroke-width: 2;
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  .art-motif-line {
    stroke-width: 3.5;
  }

  .art-motif-thin {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--art-accent);
    stroke-width: 2.5;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  .art-moon {
    stroke-width: 3;
  }

  /* The lanes of `/scale`'s motif: the same line three times, the far one faintest, so the
     picture reads as many of one thing rather than as a stack of rules. */
  .art-lane {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--art-accent);
    stroke-width: 3;
    stroke-linecap: round;
  }

  .art-lane-2 {
    opacity: 0.6;
  }

  .art-lane-3 {
    opacity: 0.35;
  }

  .art-pr-node,
  .art-pr-merge,
  .art-pr-base,
  .art-check {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--ok);
    stroke-width: 3;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  /* The base branch the pull request merges into: the one thick stroke in the drawing, which
     is what makes it read as the line everything else lands on. */
  .art-pr-base {
    stroke-width: 6;
  }

  /* One accent per tone (spec §6). Three of the four are colours the palette gives a role to;
     `/crew` takes the green again and spends its lightness on the drawing instead, because
     `--warn` means "pay attention" and belongs to the caps page. */
  .campaign-hero-art[data-tone='savings'] {
    --art-accent: var(--ok);
  }

  .campaign-hero-art[data-tone='control'] {
    --art-accent: var(--accent);
  }

  .campaign-hero-art[data-tone='scale'] {
    --art-accent: var(--accent-on-dark);
  }

  .campaign-hero-art[data-tone='playful'] {
    --art-accent: var(--ok);
  }

  /* `/crew` is the one the spec asks to be lighter: thicker, rounder strokes on the marks that
     are not the cloud, so the playful register is in the drawing rather than in a colour. */
  .campaign-hero-art[data-tone='playful'] .art-motif-line,
  .campaign-hero-art[data-tone='playful'] .art-spark {
    stroke-width: 4.5;
  }

  /* The phone's own block: the drawing is smaller than the column and centred, so the headline
     and the sentence under it keep the top of the page and the picture follows them (the
     owner's feedback of 2026-10-30). The picture is read at 375 px more than at any other
     width, and it is the block that gives way. */
  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .campaign-hero-art {
      max-width: 20rem;
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
  }

  /* The wide viewport's: the picture sits on the hero's outer edge, in the column the layout
     gives it, rather than floating in the middle of it. */
  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .campaign-hero-art {
      margin-inline-start: auto;
    }
  }

  /* The work travelling towards the pull request, and the far end of the route. The dashes and
     the ring are the drawing's own lengths rather than a duration: one period of the dash
     pattern, so the loop lands where it started and nothing jumps. */
  @keyframes art-flow {
    to {
      stroke-dashoffset: -32;
    }
  }

  @keyframes art-spin {
    to {
      stroke-dashoffset: -57;
    }
  }

  /* The float's end state is where it started, so a visitor who asked for less motion gets the
     drawing still rather than one tilted by a stopped animation (D105). */
  @keyframes art-float {
    50% {
      transform: translateY(-4px);
    }
  }

  @keyframes art-fade {
    50% {
      opacity: 0.85;
    }
  }
</style>
